# Back to Neovim (from Helix)

Your setup, your keymaps. Leader is `<Space>`.
Scroll with `j`{normal}. Lines marked ✗ turn ✓ when you fix them.
Messed up? `<Esc>`{normal} then `u`{normal} undoes. Nothing here is saved.

# The one rule

Helix is selection first: select, then act (`wd`).
Vim is verb first: act, then say on what (`dw`).

    d  delete      c  change (delete + insert)      y  yank
    w  word        $  end of line      iw  inner word      i(  inside ( )

So `dw` = delete word, `ci(` = change inside parens, `yy` = yank line.
Doubling the verb means "this line": `dd`, `cc`, `yy`.

# Helix habits that will bite

    Helix          Neovim          careful
    x              V               x in Vim DELETES a character
    xd             dd
    wd             dw / daw
    mi( c          ci(
    %              ggVG            % in Vim = matching bracket
    mm             %
    gh / gl        0 / $           ^ = first non-blank
    ge             G               ge in Vim = end of previous word
    U              <C-r>           redo
    ;              <Esc>           ; in Vim repeats f/t
    Space f        ⌘⇧P             find files   (your map)
    Space /        ⌘⇧O             live grep    (your map)
    Space b        Space fb        buffers      (your map)
    Space e        Space e         neo-tree     (same!)
    Space k        K               hover        (your map)
    Space r        grn             rename       (built in, 0.11+)
    Space a        gra             code action  (built in, 0.11+)
    gr             grr             references   (built in, 0.11+)
    Ctrl-c         gcc             comment      (Comment.nvim)

# 1.1 MOVING: h j k l and counts

You have `relativenumber` on: the gutter shows how far each line is.
Instead of `jjjjj`, read the number and type `5j`{normal}.

 1. Look at the gutter, then jump straight to the line saying TARGET with a count.

    not this one
    not this one
    not this one
    not this one
    TARGET: you got here with one count + j, right?

# 1.2 MOVING: words

`w` next word start, `e` word end, `b` back. `W E B` = space-separated WORDS.

 1. Put the cursor on `const` below. Reach `addSuffix` in as few keys as you can.
    (Hint: count the words. `10w`{normal}. Or `7W`{normal}.)

    const message = formatDistance(start, end, { addSuffix: true });

# 1.3 MOVING: within a line

`0` col 0, `^` first non-blank, `$` end. `f{c}` onto char, `t{c}` just before it.
`;` repeats the last f/t, `,` goes back.

 1. On the line below try: `^`{normal}  `f!`{normal}  `$`{normal}  `0`{normal}  `fu`{normal} `;`{normal} `;`{normal} `,`{normal}

        if (user.role === "admin" && !user.disabled) return true;

# 1.4 MOVING: big jumps

    gg / G        first / last line
    42G or :42    line 42
    %             matching bracket
    <C-d> <C-u>   half page down / up
    <C-o> <C-i>   jump back / forward (works after gg, G, /, gd, ...)
    zz            center the screen on the cursor

 1. Press `G`{normal}, then `<C-o>`{normal} to come back here.
 2. Put the cursor on the `{` below and press `%`{normal} a few times.

    if (!res.ok) { throw new Error(`failed: ${res.status}`); }

# 1.5 MOVING: search

`/text<Enter>` forward, `?text<Enter>` back, `n`/`N` next/prev, `*` word under cursor.
Clear the highlight with `:noh`{vim} or `<C-l>`{normal}.

 1. Type `/retries`{normal} `<Enter>`{normal}, then `n`{normal} `n`{normal} `N`{normal}.
 2. Put the cursor on `attempt` and press `*`{normal}.

    const retries = 3;
    while (attempt < retries) { attempt++; }
    if (attempt === retries) log("last attempt");

# 2.1 EDITING: delete

    x     delete char          dw   delete to next word
    D     delete to line end   dd   delete line      3dd  three lines
    dt]   delete till ]        di"  delete inside quotes

 1. Make the ✗ line match the line under it. Cursor on the `,` before "archived", then `dt]`{normal}.

const tags = ["draft", "published", "archived"];
const tags = ["draft", "published"];

 2. Delete these three junk lines with one command: cursor on the first, `3dd`{normal}.

console.log("junk 1");
console.log("junk 2");
console.log("junk 3");
keep me

# 2.2 EDITING: change

`c` = delete + insert mode. `ciw` inner word, `ci(` inside parens, `ci"` inside quotes,
`cw` to end of word, `C` to end of line. Finish each with `<Esc>`.
These work from ANYWHERE inside the word / parens / quotes.

 1. Make each ✗ line match the line under it.

function greet(name, greeting) {
function welcome(user) {

  return "Hello, " + name;
  return "Hi, " + user;

# 2.3 EDITING: yank and put

`yy` yank line, `p` put below, `P` put above, `yiw` yank word.
Note: in Neovim `Y` yanks to end of line (not the whole line like old Vim).

 1. Try `yy`{normal}`p`{normal} on any line, then `u`{normal}.
 2. Fix the ✗ line: cursor on `formatDistance`, `yiw`{normal}, then `j`{normal} `f(`{normal} `P`{normal}.

formatDistance
const label = (trip.km);

# 2.4 EDITING: the dot

`.` repeats your last change. Do it once, move, press `.` again.
`u` undo, `<C-r>` redo.

 1. On the first `let`: `cwconst<Esc>`{normal}. Then `j.`{normal} `j.`{normal} `j.`{normal}

let a = 1;
let b = 2;
let c = 3;
let d = 4;

# 2.5 EDITING: visual mode

`v` charwise, `V` linewise, `<C-v>` block. Then a verb: `d y c > < gc`.
`o` jumps to the other end of the selection. `gv` would normally reselect,
but YOUR config remaps `gv` to "definition in vsplit".

 1. Indent the two middle lines: cursor on `start();`, then `Vj>`{normal}.

if (ready) {
start();
log("started");
}

# 2.6 EDITING: comments (Comment.nvim)

`gcc` toggle line, `gc2j` this + 2 lines, `gc` in visual, `gbc` block comment.

 1. Comment the first line, uncomment the second (`gcc`{normal}, `j`{normal}, `gcc`{normal}).

const api = createClient();
// const legacy = createLegacyClient();

# 2.7 EDITING: substitute

Helix multi-select + replace becomes a command:

    :s/old/new/g     this line         :%s/old/new/g    whole file
    *  then  cgn new<Esc>  then  . . .  (replace next match, repeat)

 1. Turn every `foo` on the line into `bar`: `:s/foo/bar/g`{vim} `<Enter>`{normal}

foo + foo + foo
bar + bar + bar

# 3.1 YOUR SETUP: files and search

These leave the tutorial. Come back with `:Tutor msh`{vim}, or `<C-o>`{normal} / `:b#`{vim}.

    <Space>e       toggle Neo-tree (focus jumps into it)
                   in the tree: Enter open, Space fold, a add, d delete, r rename,
                   s vsplit, q close, ? all keys.  NOTE: `l` does NOT open folders.
    <C-w>h / l     focus tree / code
    ⌘⇧P            find files      (Telescope)
    ⌘⇧O            live grep       (Telescope)
    <Space>fb      open buffers    <Space>fh   help tags
    in a picker:   type to filter, <C-n>/<C-p> move, Enter open,
                   <C-v> vsplit, <C-x> split, <C-q> results to quickfix,
                   Esc once = picker normal mode (j/k), Esc twice = close

 1. `:cd ~/.config/nvim`{vim}, then `<Space>e`{normal}, open init.lua from the tree.
 2. `⌘⇧O`{normal}, type `mapleader`, Enter.
 3. `<Space>fb`{normal} and jump back to this tutor buffer.

If ⌘⇧P / ⌘⇧O do nothing, your terminal is eating ⌘. Use `:Telescope find_files`{vim}
and `:Telescope live_grep`{vim}, or add leader maps (see the end).

# 3.2 YOUR SETUP: LSP

    gd            go to definition         <C-o>   come back
    gv            definition in a vsplit (opens on the right: splitright)
    K             hover docs
    <Space>lf     format buffer
    grn           rename          gra  code action
    grr           references      gri  implementation
    gO            document symbols
    ]d / [d       next / prev diagnostic
    <C-w>d        show the diagnostic message (0.11+ doesn't print it inline)
    <C-s>         signature help (insert mode)

 1. In init.lua (lua_ls), put the cursor on `builtin` in a keymap line, `gd`{normal}, `<C-o>`{normal}.
 2. `K`{normal} on `vim.keymap.set`.

Completion (insert mode): `<Tab>`/`<S-Tab>` move, `<Enter>` accept,
`<C-Space>` open, `<C-e>` close, `<C-b>`/`<C-f>` scroll docs.

# 3.3 YOUR SETUP: the rest

    <Space>tt     toggle eyecandy light / dark
    gitsigns      blame shows on the cursor line automatically
                  `:Gitsigns preview_hunk`{vim}  `:Gitsigns reset_hunk`{vim}  (no keymaps yet)
    folds         OFF by default: press `zi`{normal} to enable, then `za zc zo zR zM`
    windows       <C-w>v vsplit, <C-w>s split, <C-w>hjkl move, <C-w>q close, <C-w>= equalize
    buffers       ]b / [b next / prev buffer (0.11+)
    :Lazy         plugins       :Mason   LSP servers       :checkhealth

# Optional: Helix-style leader maps

If you want your Helix fingers to keep working, add these to init.lua:

~~~ lua
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>f', builtin.find_files)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>/', builtin.live_grep)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>b', builtin.buffers)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>k', vim.lsp.buf.hover)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>r', vim.lsp.buf.rename)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>a', vim.lsp.buf.code_action)
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>d', builtin.diagnostics)
~~~

(`<leader>f` would shadow `<leader>fb`/`<leader>fh` with a delay, so maybe
move those to `<leader>b` and `<leader>?`.)

Done. Run `:Tutor`{vim} for the full built-in course whenever.
